Write a Java method that takes a byte array and a list of allowed classes as input and returns an object of one of the allowed classes, using Java's ObjectInputStream for deserialization. The method should handle cases where the deserialized object does not match any of the allowed classes. Additionally, the method should validate the type of the deserialized object against the provided list of allowed types. If the deserialization fails or the type is not in the allowed list, the method should throw a custom `DeserializationException` with a meaningful message.

The implementation must consider the following constraints and challenges:
- The system has a limited memory footprint, so efficiency is crucial.
- The method needs to be thread-safe to ensure it can handle concurrent calls without data corruption.
- External data sources may provide different types of objects, and you need to ensure that only specific types are deserialized.
- Performance must be optimized to handle large volumes of data efficiently.
- Integration with existing systems is required, so any changes must not break existing workflows.

```java
public class DeserializationUtil {

    public static <T> T deserialize(byte[] data, List<Class<? extends T>> allowedTypes) throws DeserializationException {
        if (allowedTypes == null || allowedTypes.isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Allowed types must be non-null and non-empty");
        }

        try (ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(new ByteArrayInputStream(data))) {
            Object obj = ois.readObject();
            if (!allowedTypes.stream().anyMatch(type -> type.isInstance(obj))) {
                throw new DeserializationException("Deserialized object is not of an allowed type: " + obj.getClass().getName());
            }
            return castToSpecificType(obj, allowedTypes);
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            throw new DeserializationException("Failed to deserialize data", e);
        }
    }

    private static <T> T castToSpecificType(Object obj, List<Class<? extends T>> allowedTypes) {
        for (Class<? extends T> type : allowedTypes) {
            if (type.isInstance(obj)) {
                return type.cast(obj);
            }
        }
        throw new IllegalStateException("No matching type found for: " + obj.getClass().getName());
    }

    public static class DeserializationException extends RuntimeException {
        public DeserializationException(String message) {
            super(message);
        }

        public DeserializationException(String message, Throwable cause) {
            super(message, cause);
        }
    }
}
```

